{"records":[{"id":"95wwrptr59","title":"Lipid availability influences ferroptosis sensitivity in cancer cells by regulating polyunsaturated fatty acid trafficking","description":"Ferroptosis is a form of cell death caused by lipid peroxidation that is emerging as a target for cancer therapy, highlighting the need to identify factors that govern ferroptosis susceptibility. Lipid peroxidation occurs primarily on phospholipids containing polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs). Here, we show that even though extracellular lipid limitation reduces cellular PUFA levels, lipid-starved cancer cells are paradoxically more sensitive to ferroptosis. Using mass spectrometry-based lipidomics with stable isotope fatty acid labeling, we show that lipid limitation induces a fatty acid trafficking pathway in which PUFAs are liberated from triglycerides to synthesize highly unsaturated PUFAs such as arachidonic and adrenic acid. These PUFAs then accumulate in phospholipids, including ether phospholipids, to promote ferroptosis sensitivity. Therefore, PUFA levels within cancer cells do not necessarily correlate with ferroptosis susceptibility. Rather, how cancer cells respond to extracellular lipid levels by trafficking PUFAs into proper phospholipid pools contributes to their sensitivity to ferroptosis.  \n\nThis data set includes uncropped Western blot images from this manuscript.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-16T17:49:29.291Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/95wwrptr59","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/95wwrptr59"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Evan","last_name":"Lien","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"R00CA255928"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"5T32CA251066-03"}],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fdmtm3pp5f","title":"EGFR-induced Phosphorylation and Subcellular Translocation of PDHE1α Promotes Tumor Immune Evasion","description":"Original imaging data for \"EGFR-induced Phosphorylation and Subcellular Translocation of PDHE1α Promotes Tumor Immune Evasion\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-26T21:20:37.614Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fdmtm3pp5f","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fdmtm3pp5f"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yajuan","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Tumor Immunology","Cell Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3c755wtx8w","title":"lipidomics dataset-Lipid droplet plasticity and dispersion dictates differential sensitivity of therapy-resistant melanoma cells to PUFA and iron-induced ferroptosis ","description":"Lipidmics data generated for the manuscript (Lipid droplet plasticity and dispersion dictates differential sensitivity of therapy-resistant melanoma cells to PUFA and iron-induced ferroptosis)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-09T05:41:57.76Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3c755wtx8w","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3c755wtx8w"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"sahar","last_name":"motamedi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lipidomics","Data Analysis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"c5r7thhjvs","title":"Original data files of paper \"TMCO1 promotes ferroptosis and ECM deposition in glaucomatous trabecular meshwork via ERK1/2 signaling\"","description":"Original data files of paper \"TMCO1 promotes ferroptosis and ECM deposition in glaucomatous trabecular meshwork via ERK1/2 signaling\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-24T07:34:35.31Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/c5r7thhjvs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/c5r7thhjvs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"yang","last_name":"zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Ophthalmology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mps96zhgwf","title":"raw data","description":"raw data for  “ATG5-mediated keratinocyte ferroptosis promotes M1 polarization of macrophages to aggravate UVB-induced skin inflammation ”","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-22T12:25:38.144Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mps96zhgwf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mps96zhgwf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"ta","last_name":"xiao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Autophagy","Skin Disease","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6rjv2vwntc","title":"PHLDA2-mediated phosphatidic acid peroxidation triggers a distinct ferroptotic response during tumor suppression","description":"Although the role of ferroptosis in killing tumor cells is well established, recent studies indicate that ferroptosis inducers also sabotage the anti-tumor immunity by killing neutrophils thus unexpectedly, stimulate tumor growth, raising a serious issue about whether ferroptosis effectively suppresses tumor development in vivo. Through genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screenings, we discover a PHLDA2-mediated ferroptosis pathway that is neither ACSL4-dependent nor requires common ferroptosis inducers. PHLDA2-mediated ferroptosis acts through peroxidation of phosphatidic acid (PA) upon high levels of ROS. ROS-induced ferroptosis is critical for tumor growth in the absence of common ferroptosis inducers; strikingly, loss of PHLDA2 abrogates ROS-induced ferroptosis and promotes tumor growth but has no obvious effect in normal tissues in both immunodeficient and immunocompetent mouse tumor models. These data demonstrate that PHLDA2-mediated phosphatidic acid peroxidation triggers a distinct ferroptosis response critical for tumor suppression and reveal, that PHLDA2-mediated ferroptosis occurs naturally in vivo without any treatment from ferroptosis inducers.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-01-24T06:16:44.851Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6rjv2vwntc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6rjv2vwntc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":"0000-0003-0222-5839"},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Tumor","Lipid Peroxidation","CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ngttmkkhkn","title":"ALDOA-mediated metabolic reprogramming is a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization in cancer","description":"Ferroptosis presents great potential for cancer therapy, either alone or in combination with classical therapy. However, inducing ferroptosis by targeting canonical ferroptosis suppressors that directly inhibit lipid peroxidation non-selectively induces ferroptosis in both cancerous and normal cells, thereby limiting its therapeutic potential. In this study, we reveal that aldolase A (ALDOA) reprograms lipid metabolism to resist ferroptosis in cancer cells and identify ALDOA as a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization. Cancer cells with ALDOA suppression exhibit increased susceptibility to ferroptosis-a response less obvious in normal cells. Mechanistically, ALDOA depletion induces significant accumulation of fructose 1,6-bisphosphate in cancer cells, thereby enhancing autophagy-dependent degradation of phospholipid-modifying enzymes. These alterations increase the ratio of phospholipids containing pro-ferroptotic polyunsaturated fatty acids over anti-ferroptotic monounsaturated fatty acids, culminating in heightened ferroptosis sensitivity. Moreover, ALDOA inhibitors selectively promote ferroptosis in cancer cells, both in vitro and in vivo. Collectively, our findings reveal that ALDOA-mediated metabolic reprogramming is a targetable vulnerability for ferroptosis sensitization in cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-27T01:37:28.511Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ngttmkkhkn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ngttmkkhkn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"pengqi","last_name":"wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"sheng","last_name":"ding","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Tsinghua-Peking Center for Life Sciences","grant_id":null},{"id":null,"name":"Beijing Natural Science Foundation","grant_id":"JQ22016"},{"id":null,"name":"New Cornerstone Investigator Program","grant_id":null},{"id":null,"name":"National Key R&D Program of China ","grant_id":"2022YFA1103704"}],"categories":["Lipidomics","Metabolomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dh9hjpsz7t","title":"PL measurements alpha syn KD 2022","description":"Based on the research studies showing an altered PUFAs plasma membrane composition upon alterations to alpha synuclein expression levels, we hypothesised that reducing alpha syn in LUHMES dopaminergic neurons may have altered the plasma membrane composition. Phospholipid molecular species were therefore measured in LUHMES cells NT, Ctrl KD, asyn KD and ACSL4 KD under basal control or 20uM Arachidonic acid (AA) conditions. Here we report a selective and prominent reduction of ether-linked phospholipids (PC and PE) upon alpha syn and ACSL4 depletion. Such reduction in ether-PL may explain the ferroptosis resistance phenotype observed.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-07-04T19:27:51.308Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dh9hjpsz7t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dh9hjpsz7t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Laura","last_name":"Mahoney Sanchez","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mass Spectrometry","Lipidomics","Phospholipids"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"h4chjzxzh2","title":"Comparative Analysis of the in-vitro Susceptibility of Salmonella Typhi to Chloramphenicol and Leaf Extracts of Moringa Oleifera","description":"This study compared the in-vitro susceptibility of Salmonella typhi to Chloramphenicol and Moringa oleifera leaf extracts. Ethanol and ether extracts of the leaves showed activity, while hot and cold water extracts did not. Chloramphenicol demonstrated higher in-vitro activity against Salmonella typhi than the leaf extracts.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-05-28T09:38:21.656Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/h4chjzxzh2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/h4chjzxzh2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Oche","last_name":"Otorkpa","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Pharmaceutical Chemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cp8cvzx22z","title":"OPA1 promotes ferroptosis by augmenting mitochondrial ROS and suppressing an integrated stress response","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent form of nonapoptotic cell death mediated by lipid peroxidation, has been implicated in the pathogenesis of multiple diseases. Subcellular organelles play pivotal roles in the regulation of ferroptosis, but the mechanisms underlying the contributions of the mitochondria remain poorly defined. Optic atrophy 1 (OPA1) is a mitochondrial dynamin-like GTPase that controls mitochondrial morphogenesis, fusion, and energetics. Here, we report that human and mouse cells lacking OPA1 are markedly resistant to ferroptosis. Reconstitution with OPA1 mutants demonstrates that ferroptosis sensitization requires the GTPase activity but is independent of OPA1-mediated mitochondrial fusion. Mechanistically, OPA1 confers susceptibility to ferroptosis by maintaining mitochondrial homeostasis and function, which contributes both to the generation of mitochondrial lipid reactive oxygen species (ROS) and suppression of an ATF4-mediated integrated stress response. Together, these results identify an OPA1-controlled mitochondrial axis of ferroptosis regulation and provide mechanistic insights for therapeutically manipulating this form of cell death in diseases.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-13T17:28:29.217Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cp8cvzx22z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cp8cvzx22z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Felix ","last_name":"Liang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":138130,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":190,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":138130,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":137394,"restricted":70,"embargoed":666},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2284},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1335},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":694},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":468},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":442},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":425},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":412},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":391},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":313},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":275}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":135485},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1900},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":140},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":78},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":57},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":42},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":871},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":777},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":521},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":361}]}}